## 6. JSON-Specific Implementation Guidelines

### Style
- One TC = one test method. Method name includes TC-ID (e.g., `test_DO00_valid_payload`).
- Dynamic variants: append `_dynamic` (e.g., `test_DO00_D_..._dynamic`).

### Positive Hardcoded
- Assert HTTP status exactly (no ranges).
- Assert response body structure AND specific values.
- Verify side effects (DB persistence, etc.).

### Positive Dynamic
- Generate random valid data per execution (Faker/Bogus/equivalent).
- Random data conforms to schema constraints.
- Assertions on STRUCTURE/FORMAT/EQUALITY-TO-INPUT, NOT specific values.

### Negative (Schema Violations)
- Send malformed/invalid JSON exactly as specified.
- Assert exact HTTP status (400 vs 422 vs 415).
- Assert error structure.
- Verify NO side effects.

### JSON-Syntax Negatives (SYNTAX-*)
- Send RAW STRING body with appropriate Content-Type (don't serialize through JSON lib).

### Encoding (ENCODE-*)
- Explicit byte encoding (UTF-8 BOM tests require raw bytes).

### Security (SEC-*)
- Prototype pollution: after request, verify Object.prototype not modified.
- Injection: assert payload treated as data, not executed.

### Assertion Strength
- STRONG (exact value) — hardcoded tests with BR
- ADEQUATE (type/format) — unpredictable values (UUID, timestamp, dynamic)
- For DYNAMIC — input/output equality + structure
- NEVER weak ("not null") for BR-tied tests

## 7. Expected Behavior Summary
[Per test category]

## 8. Requirement Traceability Matrix
| TC-ID | Requirement | Catalog Origin | Data Strategy | Expected Source | Confidence |
